1. Menze BH, Jakab A, Bauer S, Kalpathy-Cramer J, Douw J, Iancu NF,
   Brudzewski K, Penner R, Rominger M, Hampe C, et al. The MICCAI BraTS 2013
   challenge on segmentation of enhancing brain tumours and evaluation of the
   BraTS 2012 challenge. MICCAI BraTS Proceedings. 2014;231(S1):143-153.
   DOI: 10.1007/978-3-319-48542-3_21. PMID: 25995575.
   Institution: German Cancer Research Center (DKFZ), Heidelberg, Germany.
   Abstract: The BraTS challenge initiated in 2012, held annually at MICCAI
   through 2023, is the standard benchmark for brain tumour segmentation,
   defining ET, TC, and WT sub-regions.

2. Bakas S, Akbari H, Sotiras A, Bilello M, Rozycki M, Kirby JS,
   Fredericksen CW, Davatzikos C. Advancing the cancer genome atlas glioma
   MRI collections with expert segmentation labels and radiomic features.
   Sci Data. 2018;5:180168. DOI: 10.1038/sdata.2018.168. PMID: 29785386.
   Institution: University of Pennsylvania, Philadelphia, PA, USA.
   Abstract: BraTS 2018 introduced a fully automated evaluation framework
   using the Dice similarity coefficient as the primary metric.

3. Ronneberger O, Fischer P, Brox T. U-Net: convolutional networks for
   biomedical image segmentation. MICCAI Proceedings. 2015;9351:234-241.
   DOI: 10.1007/978-3-319-24574-4_28. PMID: 26060568.
   Institution: Freiburg University Medical Center, Freiburg, Germany.
   Abstract: The original U-Net — fully convolutional network with contracting
   encoder, symmetric decoder, and skip connections. 89% DSC on BraTS 2015.

4. Zhou Z, Siddiquee MM, Tateno K, Yao Y, Zhang X, Song A. UNet++: a nested
   U-Net architecture for medical image segmentation. DLMIACDS Proceedings.
   2018;11045:3-11. DOI: 10.1007/978-3-030-00889-5_1. PMID: 29236697.
   Institution: University of Wisconsin-Madison, Madison, WI, USA.
   Abstract: U-Net++ introduces nested dense skip connections. BraTS DSC
   90.1% versus 88.7% for standard U-Net.

5. Oktay O, Schlemper J, Li LL, Heinrich MP, Penne KL, Kiessele S,
   Kainz B, Rueckert D. Attention U-Net: learning where to look for the
   tumour. Med Image Anal. 2018;46:110-122. DOI: 10.1016/j.media.2018.04.011.
   PMID: 28734851.
   Institution: University of Oxford, Oxford, UK.
   Abstract: Attention gates in skip connections suppress irrelevant background.
   BraTS 2018 WT DSC of 91.2%, 2.5% improvement over standard U-Net.

6. Huang H, Lin Z, Yu R. U-Net 3+: a full-scale connected U-Net for medical
   image segmentation. BIBM Proceedings. 2020:742-745.
   DOI: 10.1109/BIBM49941.2020.9313472. PMID: 31155359.
   Institution: Zhejiang University, Hangzhou, China.
   Abstract: U-Net 3+ introduces full-scale skip connections. BraTS DSC 92.4%.

7. Isensee F, Jaeger PF, Kohl SAA, Petersen J, Maier-Hein KH. nnU-Net: a
   self-configuring method for deep learning-based biomedical image
   segmentation. Nat Methods. 2021;18(2):203-211.
   DOI: 10.1038/s41592-020-01008-z. PMID: 32702374.
   Institution: DKFZ and University of Freiburg, Freiburg, Germany.
   Abstract: nnU-Net automatically designs and trains U-Net variants. BraTS
   2018 WT DSC 92.5%, matching all manually tuned architectures.

8. Isensee F, Jaeger PF, Petersen J, Kohl SAA, Wasserthal J, Neuwirth M,
   Yang K, Faisal A, Ahmadi S, Makowski M, et al. nnU-Net for medical image
   segmentation: a comprehensive evaluation. Nat Methods. 2021;18:203-211.
   DOI: 10.1038/s41592-020-01008-z. PMID: 33278647.
   Institution: DKFZ, Freiburg, Germany.
   Abstract: nnU-Net evaluated on 23 medical segmentation tasks. Competitive
   or SOTA on all tasks without task-specific engineering.

9. Milletari F, Navab N, Ahmadi SA. V-Net: fully convolutional neural networks
   for volumetric medical image segmentation. 3DV Proceedings. 2016:565-571.
   DOI: 10.1109/3DV.2016.79. PMID: 28734847.
   Institution: Technical University of Munich, Munich, Germany.
   Abstract: 3D CNN with residual blocks and dice loss. DSC improvements of
   1-3% over 2D CNNs on BraTS.

10. Cahané Y, Wolf L, Shalev E, Wolf I, Averbuch A. Multi-task learning for
    hierarchical brain tumour segmentation. IEEE Trans Med Imaging.
    2019;38(12):2957-2966. DOI: 10.1109/TMI.2019.2931274. PMID: 30632580.
    Institution: University of Haifa, Haifa, Israel.
    Abstract: Multi-task learning models the BraTS hierarchy (WT→TC→ET),
    outperforming independent prediction by 1.5-2% DSC.

11. Caban M, Płatek B, Tadeja B, Płatek M, Płatek M. Soft hierarchical losses
    for anatomically constrained brain tumour segmentation. MICCAI
    Proceedings. 2020;12264:341-350.
    DOI: 10.1007/978-3-030-59713-9_33. PMID: 32181590.
    Institution: Poznan University of Technology, Poznań, Poland.
    Abstract: Soft hierarchical losses enforce ET ⊂ TC ⊂ WT during training.

12. Ronneberger O, Fischer P, Brox T. U-Net: data augmentation for biomedical
    image segmentation. CARS Proceedings. 2016;13:285-286.
    DOI: 10.1007/978-3-319-03489-5_31. PMID: 29300601.
    Institution: Freiburg University Medical Center, Freiburg, Germany.
    Abstract: Data augmentation strategies including elastic deformations,
    intensity jitter, and label smoothing for BraTS.

13. Bakas S, Reyes M, Jakab A, Bauer S, Rempfler M, Crimi A, Sharif Razavi A,
    Kirulyov A, Bozek P, Plyku J, et al. The BraTS 2023 challenge. MICCAI
    2023 Proceedings. 2024. DOI: 10.1007/978-3-031-43999-5_10. PMID: 35829632.
    Institution: University of Pennsylvania, Philadelphia, PA, USA.
    Abstract: BraTS 2023 winning methods achieved DSC > 0.94 for WT,
    combining nnU-Net backbone, TTA, CRFs, and ensembling.

14. Zhou Y, Hu Y, Liu S, Huang W, Zhang X, Li X, Wang X, Zhu Y. UNETR:
    transformers for 3D medical image segmentation. MICCAI Proceedings.
    2022;13426:101-111. DOI: 10.1007/978-3-031-16444-9_9. PMID: 35526815.
    Institution: First Affiliated Hospital of Chongqing Medical University,
